#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388000 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0be03329-844c-5231-a459-314a1182f84b" :knowledge-point/title "Computing One Iteration of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea1c9356-842e-5d92-8135-bea38868a262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388002} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9f58b2-3be8-5cec-a05f-0bf64d195b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.750" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea1c9356-842e-5d92-8135-bea38868a262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.250" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388002} :answer-field/id #uuid "29c5daf3-5e9d-5338-8bb7-f8edfe6b91d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "923cdf8e-221d-52ca-bbeb-09cff5e2ce5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.167" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "923cdf8e-221d-52ca-bbeb-09cff5e2ce5e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.167" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388005} :answer-field/id #uuid "959430ca-fa24-5dd6-aacf-1c9eac73c2e4" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5eff49af-e401-58d3-bc83-e76a036120ac" :question/math-academy-id "q-257194" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,2]\\times [0,2]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)={x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}+x.\n$$\n\nCalculate one iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[1,\\,0]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2xy+3{y}^{2}+x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2xy-4x-2y+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+6y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4x-2y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-2x+6y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4x-2y+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-2x+6y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2y-4 & 2x-2 \\\\ 2x-2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}2y-4 & 2x-2 \\\\ 2x-2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-4x-2y+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with\n${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}-{[\\begin{aligned}2y-4 & 2x-2 \\\\ 2x-2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2xy-4x-2y+1 \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x+6y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}2(0)-4 & 2(1)-2 \\\\ 2(1)-2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(1)(0)-4(1)-2(0)+1 \\\\ (1)^{2}-2(1)+6(0)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(-4)\\cdot 6-(0)\\cdot (0)}[\\begin{aligned}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{24}[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.25 \\\\ 0.167\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2b0980-6844-5504-a642-fb855741b3be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c2b0980-6844-5504-a642-fb855741b3be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388008} :answer-field/id #uuid "15013857-3c5c-511c-967a-756d01e848fe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388007} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d311d293-a565-54a9-8138-865d073fbcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d311d293-a565-54a9-8138-865d073fbcee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388010} :answer-field/id #uuid "6db61ff5-0c8f-5c1a-b903-45fa9035b77b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db639318-ad6d-59e6-9902-e13ffa998c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db639318-ad6d-59e6-9902-e13ffa998c1a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388012} :answer-field/id #uuid "b61b395b-f600-5f59-94e9-fd5b77a9209e" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3d736caa-2251-5471-b5ca-3a5078a61925" :question/math-academy-id "q-257202" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+x.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T}.$\n\n| $[$ | $2$ | {{field-1}} | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-3$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-2}} | $-4$ | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}y-2{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}+x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6xy-4x+1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4y\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(6xy-4x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-4y) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(6xy-4x+1) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{x}^{2}-4y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6y-4 & 6x \\\\ 6x & -4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}6y-4 & 6x \\\\ 6x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-4x+1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}6y-4 & 6x \\\\ 6x & -4\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}6xy-4x+1 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-4y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}6(1)-4 & 6(1) \\\\ 6(1) & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}6(1)(1)-4(1)+1 \\\\ 3(1)^{2}-4(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}2 & 6 \\\\ 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7dace5-2524-570f-ab34-b896213739a2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.563" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388015} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9543d0-c0d4-5297-9cd0-a9845308aa17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b9543d0-c0d4-5297-9cd0-a9845308aa17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.438" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388016} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0671847-3baa-5f2b-bf59-3d57412d4d9c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "abcbda38-6983-5ef5-a313-614064c80e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-257211" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,1]\\times [-1,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=7{x}^{2}y-2xy+3{y}^{3}-1.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[-1,\\,1]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[-1,\\,0]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to **three decimal places.**\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | {{field-1}} | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(7{x}^{2}y-2xy+3{y}^{3}-1) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{x}^{2}y-2xy+3{y}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14xy-2y \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(14xy-2y) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(14xy-2y) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}14y & 14x-2 \\\\ 14x-2 & 18y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}14y & 14x-2 \\\\ 14x-2 & 18y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}14xy-2y \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}14y & 14x-2 \\\\ 14x-2 & 18y\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}14xy-2y \\\\ 7{x}^{2}-2x+9{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}14(0) & 14(-1)-2 \\\\ 14(-1)-2 & 18(0)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}14(-1)(0)-2(0) \\\\ 7(-1)^{2}-2(-1)+9(0)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}0 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 0\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{0\\cdot 0-(-16)\\cdot (-16)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{256}[\\begin{aligned}144 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.438 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3db0791-f73d-55e7-9b5a-f8faa62b8820" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method Using Linear Systems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "887e49bf-33d1-5d98-b2dc-41233ff0b5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "887e49bf-33d1-5d98-b2dc-41233ff0b5ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388019} :answer-field/id #uuid "41c71dc5-edfa-5be6-a520-6a86d7899505" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388018} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddb6a96-7fdc-5c8a-8cd4-f4202334f8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1ddb6a96-7fdc-5c8a-8cd4-f4202334f8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388021} :answer-field/id #uuid "c79946ba-8a29-5791-9300-2efbdddddf28" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388020} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac3a771a-ddf0-5a81-9da0-c7c31c6538e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac3a771a-ddf0-5a81-9da0-c7c31c6538e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388023} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0519ae7-21a6-55ba-9478-cb51406813a9" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388022} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "53088c52-43a0-5226-97db-040f9a7d8645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.531" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53088c52-43a0-5226-97db-040f9a7d8645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.531" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388025} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd6d62-af30-5061-bb29-8edb0b12dae3" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81767788-89b8-5d11-88b5-c9753d5eaeda" :question/math-academy-id "q-257212" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[0,1]\\times [0,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=4{x}^{2}y-3x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\nFill in the blanks in the matrix equation below that defines the first iteration of the Newton-Raphson process starting at the point $[1,\\,1]^{T},$ and compute the components of the first iteration. Round the answer for ${x}_{1}$ to **three decimal places**.\n\n| $[$ | {{field-1}} | $2$ | $]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})\\,=[$ | $-5$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |  |  |  |\n\n| ${x}_{1}≈[$ | $0.594$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-4}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nEquivalently, at each iteration, the above equation can be rewritten as\n\n$$\n{H}_{f}({x}_{n})({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-∇f({x}_{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}y-3x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}y-3x{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}-2x)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8xy-3{y}^{2}+2x-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8xy-3{y}^{2}+2x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4{x}^{2}-6xy) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(8xy-3{y}^{2}+2x-2) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4{x}^{2}-6xy)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8y+2 & 8x-6y \\\\ 8x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson system of equations is\n\n$$\n{[\\begin{aligned}8y+2 & 8x-6y \\\\ 8x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}({x}_{n+1}-{x}_{n})=-{[\\begin{aligned}8xy-3{y}^{2}+2x-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nStarting with ${x}_{0}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{[\\begin{aligned}8y+2 & 8x-6y \\\\ 8x-6y & -6x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}}({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-{[\\begin{aligned}8xy-3{y}^{2}+2x-2 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-6xy\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{0}} \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}8(1)+2 & 8(1)-6(1) \\\\ 8(1)-6(1) & -6(1)\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =-[\\begin{aligned}8(1)(1)-3(1)^{2}+2(1)-2 \\\\ 4(1)^{2}-6(1)(1)\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{aligned}]({x}_{1}-{x}_{0}) & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system of equations, we get $({x}_{1}-{x}_{0})=[\\begin{aligned}-13/32 \\\\ -15/32\\end{aligned}].$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & ={x}_{0}+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{32} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{32}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{13}{32} \\\\ -\\frac{15}{32}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}0.594 \\\\ 0.531\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a19923a-5050-5417-8c83-5fc085a81f0f" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Two Iterations of the Newton-Raphson Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94aabb8b-8d69-5cd7-a2e5-610d6615c994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.442" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94aabb8b-8d69-5cd7-a2e5-610d6615c994" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.442" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388028} :answer-field/id #uuid "665958b3-ea3c-573c-8452-552b59da85ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388027}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d166bb98-8bfa-5706-99d6-debffca7b6ab" :question/math-academy-id "q-257249" :question/problem "Suppose we wish to find a critical point of the function $f(x,y)$ in the region $[-1,2]\\times [-2,1]⊆{R}^{2},$ where\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nCalculate the second iteration of the Newton-Raphson process for optimization that starts at ${x}_{0}=[2,\\,-2]^{T}$ if the first iteration of the algorithm gives ${x}_{1}=[1.75,\\,0.125]^{T}.$ Round your final answer to three decimal places (if needed).\n\n| ${x}_{2}≈[$ | $-0.301$ | $]$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| {{field-1}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "The Newton-Raphson method of optimizing a multivariable function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-[{H}_{f}({x}_{n})]^{-1}∇f({x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nwhere $H({x}_{n})$ and $∇f({x}_{n})$ are the Hessian and gradient of $f$, respectively, evaluated at ${x}_{n}.$\n\nIn our case, the gradient is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}y+3{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the Hessian is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{H}_{f}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy-{x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy-{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our Newton-Raphson formula is\n\n$$\n{x}_{n+1}={x}_{n}-{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nFor the second iteration, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}-{[\\begin{aligned}-2y+6 & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}}^{-1}{[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2xy+6x \\\\ 4xy-{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}-2(0.125)+6 & 4(0.125)-2(1.75) \\\\ 4(0.125)-2(1.75) & 4(1.75)\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2(0.125)^{2}-2(1.75)(0.125)+6(1.75) \\\\ 4(1.75)(0.125)-(1.75)^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-{[\\begin{aligned}5.75 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}10.093\\,75 \\\\ -2.187\\,5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{(5.75)\\cdot 7-(-3)\\cdot (-3)}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 5.75\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10.093\\,75 \\\\ -2.187\\,5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1.75 \\\\ 0.125\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{31.25}[\\begin{aligned}64.093\\,75 \\\\ 17.703\\,125\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ≈[\\begin{aligned}-0.301 \\\\ -0.442\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
